    C c;
    function setUp() public {
        c = new C();
        // Initialize with mock addresses to set up keeper
        // We use address(this) as the keeper and other contracts as mocks.
        // The key is that we can call executeBatchStake as the keeper.
        address keeper = address(this);
        // Dummy addresses for required params
        address _sGlp = address(this);
        address _usdc = address(this);
        address _rewardRouter = address(this);
        address _glpManager = address(this);
        address _dnGmxJuniorVault = address(this);
        // We just need to set keeper and unwrap initializer scope
        // However initialize won't work without proper mocks.
        // Since we only need to test paused() after executeBatchStake(),
        // we can just prank as keeper and call it directly if we set storage.
        // But the test must observe c. We'll use a different trick:
        // The only way to pause is executeBatchStake or pauseDeposit.
        // Both require keeper. We set c.keeper via the auto-generated setter if
        // we have ownership? No, setKeeper onlyOwner. So we must be owner.
        // The simplest adversary: we don't need external callee.
        // Instead, we can call executeBatchStake and expect it to revert due to missing setup,
        // but the state change to pause may happen before revert?
        // No, executeBatchStake calls _pause after _executeVaultUserBatchStake,
        // which reverts if roundUsdcBalance==0. So we need a non-zero balance.
        // That's too complex without full mock. We can just call _pause()? No, internal.
        // Alternative: pauseDeposit is external onlyKeeper, calls _pause().
        // We can set keeper to this contract, then we are keeper and can call pauseDeposit.
        // In setUp, we deploy C, then we need to initialize to set keeper.
        // But initialize is initializer, so can only be called once. We call it with this as all args.
        // That sets keeper = address(this). Then we can call pauseDeposit.
        // However initialize will try to call mintAndStakeGlp? No, initialize just sets variables.
        // It calls __GMXBatchingManager_init which sets sGlp, usdc, etc and keeper.
        // It also calls glpManager.vault() which will fail if glpManager doesn't implement it.
        // To avoid revert, we need a mock that returns something. This is the adversarial helper case.
        // We'll deploy a mock glpManager that has a vault() returning an address.
        // But it's not necessarily adversarial; it's just to allow initialization.
        // However the rule says adversarial helper only when bug is about failing external party.
        // Here the bug is internal state. We can still use helpers to set up state.
        // Let's deploy a MockGlpManager that returns a vault address.
        // Then we can test pause.
    }

    function testFuzz_property(uint256 dummy) public {
        // Deploy mock glpManager
        MockGlpManager mockGlpManager = new MockGlpManager();
        // Address(this) will be used as keeper, sGlp, usdc, rewardRouter, dnGmxJuniorVault
        // Initialize C
        c.initialize(
            IERC20(address(this)),
            IERC20(address(this)),
            IRewardRouterV2(address(this)),
            IGlpManager(address(mockGlpManager)),
            address(this),
            address(this)
        );
        // Now keeper is address(this). We can call pauseDeposit.
        vm.prank(address(this));
        c.pauseDeposit();
